\paragraph{Aggregation and Deployment.}
At $K{=}3$, Bayesian calibration reverses $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s direction (6/7 significant coefficients negative) by downweighting weak judges via calibrated confusion matrices (Table~\ref{tab:aggregation}); at $K{=}5$, Bayesian aggregation neutralizes both predictors ($\eta_{\max}$ 1/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 0/12; Table~\ref{tab:aggregation_k5}), eliminating panel composition's predictive power entirely. Robustness-first selection ($\min \eta_{\max}$) achieves lowest ASR in 12/12 conditions at $K{=}3$ (Appendix~\ref{app:panel_selection}). Practitioners should match strategy to panel size: screen for individual robustness at $K{=}3$; prefer correlated robustness profiles at $K{\geq}5$. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is computed from clean pairwise MI; recomputing MI under attacked conditions confirms stability (Spearman $\rho{\geq}0.91$ for 4/6 attacks), though position bias substantially disrupts MI structure ($\rho{=}0.08$ on ARC), consistent with its anomalous regression behavior (Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}). Fractional logit regression \citep{papke1996econometric} yields 93.1\% directional agreement with OLS and preserves the transition pattern (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_checks}). The random-fault contrast (\S\ref{sec:rf_contrast}) shows $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ predicts under non-adaptive noise ($R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$), with the predictability collapse under adaptive attack most pronounced at $K{=}3$ (1/12) and attenuated at $K{\geq}5$ (7/12).
